{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And Miriam the prophetess took, etc.</b> Now she became a prophetess, for at this station the women too merited to see the face of the Shekhinah, so that all of them said, ״זֶה אֵלִי״, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Mekhilta, Beshallah, section 3), 'A maidservant saw at the sea,' etc. Therefore it says, ״וַתֵּצֶאןָ כָּל הַנָּשִׁים אַחֲרֶיהָ״, for prophecy began with Miriam and all the women followed in her footsteps at this station, for all of them merited prophecy. And since the Shekhinah does not rest except out of joy, and women have the pain of childbirth, therefore she took the timbrel in her hand, and all the women went out after her with timbrels and with מְחֹלוֹת, in order that the spirit of holiness might rest upon them out of joy." "And as for what is written, <b>״אֲחוֹת אַהֲרֹן״ — the sister of Aaron.</b> Because she was equal to him in prophecy, but not to Moses, for so the plain meaning suggests at the end of parashat Beha'alotekha in the verse, ״לֹא כֵן עַבְדִּי מֹשֶׁה״ (Numbers 12:7), for there Aaron and Miriam were equated in prophecy; and so Maharil explained. And the word מְחֹלוֹת carries the meaning of forgiveness of sin, and so Rabbenu Bachya wrote. And perhaps this is what is concluded in the Yalkut on this parashah under the verse ״וַיַּסַּע מֹשֶׁה״ (Exodus 15:22), that anyone for whom a miracle is performed and who says song — it is known that all his sins are forgiven him; and from where does one learn to say so, if not from the word מְחֹלוֹת, which is mentioned in the song of these women?" "<b>And as for what it says, ״וַתַּעַן לָהֶם מִרְיָם — And Miriam answered them.״</b> It should have said לָהֶן [the feminine form]. But because at the sea the women attained the level of the men in the grasp of prophecy, therefore ״לָהֶם״ is said, as one speaking to males; and so too for the future it is said (Jeremiah 31:22), ״נְקֵבָה תְּסוֹבֵב גָּבֶר״."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {1 "Maharil likely refers to Rabbi Jacob ben Judah Weil, though it could potentially refer to Rabbi Jacob Moellin; resolved by context as a standard rabbinic citation form."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:43.550089279Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}